      "claim_text": "Both the exec and the HTTP liveness examples restart the container when the probe fails.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 0 states that if the exec command returns non-zero, the kubelet kills and restarts the container. Passage 1 states that if the HTTP handler returns a failure code, the kubelet kills and restarts the container.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 0, "quote": "If the command returns a non-zero value, the kubelet kills the container and restarts it."},
        {"index": 1, "quote": "If the handler returns a failure code, the kubelet kills the container and restarts it."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The exec example probes every 5 seconds.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 0 explicitly states that the periodSeconds field specifies a liveness probe every 5 seconds.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 0, "quote": "The `periodSeconds` field specifies that the kubelet should perform a liveness probe every 5 seconds."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The HTTP example probes every 3 seconds.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 1 explicitly states that the periodSeconds field specifies a liveness probe every 3 seconds.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 1, "quote": "The `periodSeconds` field specifies that the kubelet should perform a liveness probe every 3 seconds."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "In the TCP example the readiness and liveness probes both start 15 seconds after the container starts.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 2 says the first liveness probe runs 15 seconds after container start; passage 3 says the first readiness probe runs 15 seconds after container start.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 2, "quote": "The kubelet will run the first liveness probe 15 seconds after the container starts."},
        {"index": 3, "quote": "The kubelet will run the first readiness probe 15 seconds after the container starts."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "In the TCP example both probes connect to the goproxy container on port 8080.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 2 states the liveness probe attempts to connect to the goproxy container on port 8080; passage 3 states the readiness probe similarly attempts to connect to the goproxy container on port 8080.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 2, "quote": "This will attempt to connect to the `goproxy` container on port 8080."},
        {"index": 3, "quote": "Similar to the liveness probe, this will attempt to connect to the `goproxy` container on port 8080."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "To delay readiness checks you can use initialDelaySeconds or a startup probe.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 6 explicitly states that to wait before executing a readiness probe, you should use initialDelaySeconds or a startupProbe.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 6, "quote": "If you want to wait before executing a readiness probe, you should use `initialDelaySeconds` or a `startupProbe`."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Readiness does not wait for liveness.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 6 states that readiness and liveness probes do not depend on each other to succeed.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 6, "quote": "The readiness and liveness probes do not depend on each other to succeed."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The startup probe in the docs gives up after 300 seconds.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 5 states that the application has a maximum of 5 minutes (300s) to finish startup, and if the startup probe never succeeds, the container is killed after 300s.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 5, "quote": "the application will have a maximum of 5 minutes (30 * 10 = 300s) to finish its startup."},
        {"index": 5, "quote": "If the startup probe never succeeds, the container is killed after 300s"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "An application that needs six minutes to start would be killed before it ever became ready.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages do not state what happens to an application needing six minutes to start; they only describe the 300-second limit for the startup probe example. The claim about being killed before becoming ready is not directly entailed.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Using readiness and liveness together keeps traffic away from containers that are not ready while still restarting containers that fail.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 7 states that using both can ensure traffic does not reach a container that is not ready and that containers are restarted when they fail.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 7, "quote": "Using both can ensure that traffic does not reach a container that is not ready for it, and that containers are restarted when they fail."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "In the HTTP example the container is restarted once the checks start failing after 10 seconds.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 8 mentions health checks failing after 10 seconds and the kubelet killing and restarting the container, but it does not specify that this is the HTTP example. The HTTP example is described in passage 1, which does not mention a 10-second failure time.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The 10-second failure time is five times the probe period of 3 seconds.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages do not state that the 10-second failure time is five times the probe period of 3 seconds. Passage 8 mentions 10 seconds but does not relate it to a 3-second period.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
